Use Case in Expense Tracker

Without JWT:
Problem: Every time a user performs an action (e.g., adding an expense), you would need to re-check their credentials (email/password). This is inefficient and less secure.

With JWT:
Solution: After a user logs in, the server sends a token that the client uses in subsequent requests. You validate the token to allow or deny access.
Example: Only logged-in users can:
View their expenses.
Add new expenses.
Delete expenses.

How JWT Works in Your Expense Tracker
Login:

User logs in using email and password.
Server generates a token and sends it back to the client.
Token contains user info (e.g., id, email) in a secure, encoded form.
Subsequent API Requests:

The client includes the token in the Authorization header (Bearer <token>).
The server validates the token using the secret key and allows access.
Protected Routes:

Routes like GET /expenses or POST /expenses are protected using middleware.
Middleware checks if the token is valid before allowing the request to proceed.


Why You Don’t Need to Import the User Model
The ref field simply tells Mongoose which model to refer to. Mongoose already knows about the User model because you've registered it using mongoose.model('User', userSchema).

When you use populate, Mongoose automatically fetches the User data based on the reference.
